sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2015-girardia-09> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "In an agent-based model of planarian head regeneration in which static somatic cells emit a chemical signal and migratory cells (neoblasts or their progeny) are repelled by it, deactivating different subsets of signaling cells (representing octanol gap-junction blockade) reproduces the four observed head shapes, so a small set of gap-junction-regulated cell behaviors such as migration and cell-cell signaling can quantitatively explain the observed morphologies.";
    dc:type "simulation-result";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Agent-based model of migrating, signaling cells reproduces the observed head shapes";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Emmons-Bell M, Durant F, Hammelman J, Bessonov N, Volpert V, Morokuma J, Pinet K, Adams DS, Pietak A, Lobo D, Levin M, “Gap Junctional Blockade Stochastically Induces Different Species-Specific Head Anatomies in Genetically Wild-Type Girardia dorotocephala Flatworms”, International journal of molecular sciences (2015), doi:10.3390/ijms161126065; section “2. Results › 2.8. A Model of Planarian Head Shapes Arising from Cell Interactions”. Scope: The authors' two-cell-type agent-based model of the planarian head with an elastic boundary"
}
